d and you can buy them  that doesn't mean that they're not  regulated so they still have to be safe  they can't contain  banned ingredients and they still have  to be stable and uncontaminated  this is the case in most places around  the world and this applies to  medical grade skin care and drugstore  skin care products  there's no legal difference between the  two categories on the other hand we have  drugs and when it comes to skin care  products this is over-the-counter drugs  or prescription drugs i think a lot of  the confusion comes from the use of the  phrase  over-the-counter people promoting  medical grade skin care products often  use this term to describe  products that you buy at the drugstore  and they say that  over-the-counter products are less  effective than medical grade products  in drug regulation over-the-counter has  a legal meaning  it refers to drugs that you can buy  without a prescription  and so over-the-counter drugs are  actually more strictly regulated than  most medical grade skin care  over-the-counter drugs include things  like sunscreen  aspirin benzoyl peroxide cream and  antihistamines  this is in contrast to a prescription  drug where you need to have a  prescription from an appropriately  authorized health professional  like a doctor before you're allowed to  buy it both over-the-counter drugs and  prescription drugs need to be approved  before they're allowed to be sold  and part of that approval process is  showing that it is effective  there are also different standards for  how drugs need to be manufactured  compared to cosmetics  products that are regulated as drugs  will be clearly labeled so in the us for  example there's the drug facts panel  in australia there will be the ost r or  the host l number  as well as the concentrations of the  active ingredients on the front  in some countries like japan and korea  there's also the quasi-drug or  cosmeceutical category  and that's also regulated a bit  differently from both 