nd lots of clinical  studies on their products as well  even the really large mass market brands  that a lot of people promoting medical  grade products tend to diss  like olay and neutrogena they conduct a  lot of clinical studies and scientific  research that goes into making their  products  elay are responsible for a lot of the  information we know about niacinamide in  skin care neutrogena do a lot of studies  on sunscreens  and both of these brands publish a lot  of their research in leading  peer-reviewed scientific journals  on the other hand it's really rare that  you see a medical grade skincare brand  publish peer-reviewed scientific  research  they contain higher percentages of  actives they're allowed to penetrate  deeper than over-the-counter and  drugstore products  there are also these myths about the  effectiveness of the products  so medical grade skin care products are  supposedly allowed to put in higher  concentrations of actives  or they're allowed to penetrate deeper  into the skin and so the deeper it goes  the bigger the effect it can have  but again this is a myth because medical  grade has no legal meaning  so there's no regulatory difference and  so there's nothing that they are allowed  to do that regular skin care isn't  allowed to do  you can get really high percentages of  actors from brands like paula's choice  in the ordinary  and these are definitely not medical  grade skincare i think the myth  of medical grade skin care products  being the one true skincare that we  should all be using  is mostly perpetuated by people who have  good intentions  but they've been fed the wrong  information by brands who are either  being dishonest  or should really know better and that's  okay we've all fallen for marketing  myths before they can be really  convincing especially if they're coming  from someone who appears to be really  authoritative and knowledgeable but  there's also a small but very vocal  group of people online who are using  th